= Tutorials

== Purpose

This section contains step-by-step tutorials to help you learn Ceradoc through hands-on examples. Each tutorial builds on previous concepts and includes runnable code.

== Overview

Tutorials are organized by skill level and topic:

* link:/ceradoc/tutorials/basic-parsing/[Basic Parsing] - Parse your first AsciiDoc document
* link:/ceradoc/tutorials/document-modeling/[Document Modeling] - Work with the document model
* link:/ceradoc/tutorials/serialization/[Serialization] - Serialize models to AsciiDoc
* link:/ceradoc/tutorials/round-tripping/[Round-tripping] - Preserve semantics through parse cycles
* link:/ceradoc/tutorials/html-conversion/[HTML Conversion] - Convert HTML to AsciiDoc

== Tutorial Structure

Each tutorial follows this structure:

* **Purpose** - What you'll learn
* **Prerequisites** - What you should know first
* **Examples** - Step-by-step runnable code
* **Exercises** - Practice what you learned
* **Next Steps** - What to learn next

== Learning Path

We recommend this order for learning Ceradoc:

. link:/ceradoc/tutorials/basic-parsing/[Basic Parsing] - Start here if you're new
. link:/ceradoc/tutorials/document-modeling/[Document Modeling] - Understand the model
. link:/ceradoc/tutorials/serialization/[Serialization] - Generate AsciiDoc output
. link:/ceradoc/tutorials/round-tripping/[Round-tripping] - Learn about preservation
. link:/ceradoc/tutorials/html-conversion/[HTML Conversion] - Advanced conversion

== Example Code

All code examples in tutorials are runnable. You can execute them by:

[source,ruby]
----
# Save to a file (e.g., tutorial.rb)
# Then run with: ruby tutorial.rb

require 'ceradoc'

# Paste the example code here
----

